  "planFlaws": [
   "All six perspectives depend on the same retrieval path: abstracts and landing pages. None required reading the results tables or official documents, which is where corrected elasticities and CBO numbers are. So every perspective shared one blind spot, and the central numeric question could not be answered by design.",
   "Checklist item 1 lists the Dube and Zipperer own-wage-elasticity repository among the 'publication-bias-corrected meta-analyses'. It is an uncorrected collection of estimates. This framing let the report present an uncorrected median (-0.13) as the 'best current estimate'.",
   "Item 1 of the scoper's checklist puts the Dube and Zipperer OWE repository among the 'central employment elasticities' and uses one set of bands (-0.05 and -0.2) for all of them. It never says whether the bands apply to elasticities with respect to the minimum wage or to own-wage elasticities. An OWE is usually several times larger in size than the minimum-wage elasticity for the same data. This is why the report compares an OWE of -0.13 with minimum-wage elasticities of -0.01 to -0.06, and why claim [24] puts -0.13 into a band that was probably meant for the other unit. Item 3's 0.1 threshold has the same problem.",
   "The disemployment steelman perspective produced no number. The only evidence from the Neumark and Wascher side is one abstract [3][10][23]. The plan did not require a numeric estimate from each side before weighing them, so the result leans to one side because one side's numbers are missing, not because the evidence was weighed.",
   "The hypothesis bands (at or above -0.05, between -0.05 and -0.2, at or below -0.2) do not say which elasticity they measure: employment with respect to the minimum wage, or own-wage. The two scales differ by roughly the wage pass-through factor, so one band can hold very different effects. The report then puts a -0.13 own-wage elasticity next to -0.01 to -0.06 minimum-wage elasticities as if they were comparable.",
   "The perspective set did not guard against depending on one author. Of the 26 claims, 14 come from Dube's own work (the repository, ADRZ 2017, the 2019 review). The official-forecaster perspective settled on Dube's review for HM Treasury, which is an academic synthesis from the Card and Krueger side, not a scoring agency. No perspective had to get CBO or German Minimum Wage Commission documents.",
   "The scoper gave no dedicated sub-question to the literal Card and Krueger versus Neumark and Wascher exchange (New Jersey and Pennsylvania, 1994 and 2000). It is only a sub-bullet, and the research dropped it without any signal."
  ],
